    title: "Google Business Profile Categories & Local SEO Citations: The Chennai Small Business Checklist",
    excerpt:
      "Picking the wrong Google Business Profile category, or having your business name and phone number listed differently across the web, quietly caps how well you can rank locally. Here's how to fix both.",
    date: "August 25, 2026",
    author: {
      name: "Priyanka D.",
      role: "Founder, My DP Digital",
      avatar:
        "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534528741775-53994a69daeb?w=120&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80",
    },
    category: "SEO",
    readingTime: "6 min read",
    commentsCount: 0,
    tags: ["Google Business Profile", "local SEO", "citations", "Chennai"],
    featuredImgColor: "from-[#3B82F6] to-[#B026FF]",
    content: [
      "Most local SEO advice stops at \"claim your Google Business Profile and add photos.\" That's necessary but incomplete — two details that get overlooked constantly, even by businesses that have had a profile for years, are the category chosen and whether your business is listed consistently across the rest of the web. Both quietly cap how well you can rank in the local map pack, regardless of how good your website is.",
      "### Why Your Primary Category Choice Matters More Than Most Owners Think",
      "Google leans heavily on your primary category to decide which searches your business is even eligible to show up for. A clinic listed as \"Medical Center\" when it's actually a dermatology-specific practice competes against every general clinic nearby, instead of the much smaller, more relevant \"Dermatologist\" pool where it would rank higher with less effort. The most specific accurate category available almost always beats a broader, safer-sounding one.",
      "### Secondary Categories: Use Them, But Don't Stuff Them",
      "Secondary categories let you show up for reasonably related searches beyond your primary one — a bakery that also does custom cakes might add \"Cake shop\" alongside \"Bakery.\" The mistake is adding every loosely related category available; Google can interpret an over-stuffed category list as an attempt to game the system, and it dilutes rather than strengthens your relevance signal.",
      "### What a \"Citation\" Actually Is",
      "A citation is any online mention of your business name, address, and phone number, whether or not it links back to your website — a directory listing, a mention in a local news article, a chamber of commerce member list. Google uses the consistency and volume of these mentions as a trust signal for local rankings, separate from your website's own SEO.",
      "### Where Chennai Businesses Should Actually Get Listed",
      "Beyond Google Business Profile itself, the highest-value citations are usually: Justdial and Sulekha (still heavily used in Indian local search), industry-specific directories relevant to your business type, your own local chamber of commerce or trade association if one exists, and any legitimate local news or blog coverage you can genuinely earn. Quality and consistency matter more than sheer quantity — twenty accurate, consistent listings beat a hundred sloppy ones.",
      "### The NAP Consistency Problem (Name, Address, Phone)",
      "\"NAP consistency\" means your business Name, Address, and Phone number should read identically everywhere they appear online — the same abbreviations, the same phone format, the same suite or floor number. A business listed as \"DP Digital Pvt Ltd\" on one directory and \"D.P. Digital\" on another, or with an old phone number still live somewhere, sends Google a small but real signal of unreliable data, which works against local ranking trust. This is tedious to audit but straightforward to fix once you find the inconsistencies.",
      "- Is your Google Business Profile primary category the most specific accurate option, not just the safest-sounding one?\n- Have you added secondary categories that are genuinely relevant, without stuffing in loosely related ones?\n- Are you listed on the directories that actually matter for your industry and location, not just generic ones?\n- Does your business name, address, and phone number read identically across every listing you can find?\n- Do you still have any old address or phone number live on a forgotten directory listing?",
      "### What My DP Digital Does Differently",
      "As part of local SEO work, we audit existing citations for NAP inconsistencies, fix the primary and secondary category selection on your Google Business Profile, and build out the directory listings that are actually relevant to your specific business and location — not a generic, padded list.",
      "If you're not sure whether your business is listed consistently across the directories that matter, reach us at +91 81483 20217 or deenapriyanka00217@gmail.com and we'll take an honest look.",
    ],
