  id: "google-ads-ad-assets-guide",
  slug: "google-ads-ad-assets-guide",
  title: "Google Ads Assets: A Practical Small Business Guide",
  excerpt: "Google Ads assets add useful details, extra links or contact options to an ad. This guide explains the common types and how to choose them without repeating your ad copy.",
  content: [
    "Google Ads assets add useful information, links or contact options to an advertisement. They can help a person understand what the business offers before clicking, but they are not guaranteed to appear in every auction. The right asset is the one that clarifies the offer, supports the searcher's intent and leads to a relevant destination—not the one that simply fills every available field.",
    "### What are Google Ads assets?",
    "Assets are additional pieces of information that can be associated with an ad or campaign. Depending on the campaign, eligibility and account settings, Google may show extra links, short business details, images, phone options or lead forms alongside the main ad. Assets are not a replacement for a clear headline, description, keyword theme or landing page.",
    "Think of the main ad as the invitation and the assets as supporting details. If the headline says a business builds websites, a sitelink could help a visitor reach a WordPress page, a service overview or a contact page. Each asset should have a purpose.",
    "### Sitelink assets: send people to the right page",
    "Sitelinks provide additional clickable destinations. A small business should use pages that represent genuinely different next steps, such as a service overview, a specific service, pricing information when it is accurate and available, or a contact page. Do not use four links that all lead to the same generic page with slightly different labels.",
    "Write sitelink text that describes the destination. If the link says WordPress Website Development, the page should actually explain that service. Add descriptions when the format supports them and when they help clarify the value. Review the destination on mobile; the page should load, answer the question and offer a sensible next step.",
    "### Callout assets: add short factual details",
    "Callouts are brief, non-clickable snippets that can highlight facts about the business or offer. Use them to add information that is not already repeated in the headline or description. Examples might be a genuine delivery process, a supported service, an actual consultation format or a verified payment option.",
    "Avoid vague claims such as Best Agency, Guaranteed Results or No. 1 unless the statement is defensible and permitted. A short factual callout is easier for a visitor to evaluate than a broad marketing claim.",
    "### Structured snippets: describe what is available",
    "Structured snippets group a list of products, services or features under an eligible header. They should be descriptive rather than promotional. If the business offers website design, WordPress development and landing pages, the values should reflect the real scope of the business. Do not add services that the team does not actually provide simply because they fit a snippet.",
    "Check the current asset format and header options in Google Ads before building the list. The platform may change the labels or availability.",
    "### Call and lead form assets",
    "Call assets make a phone action available where eligible. They are useful only if the business can answer or follow up reliably. Check the phone number, hours, call tracking setup and what happens when a call is missed. If phone calls matter, measure qualified calls rather than treating every tap as a sale.",
    "Lead form assets allow a person to submit information within the ad experience. Choose questions that are useful for qualification without asking for unnecessary sensitive information. A form that asks for too much may discourage submissions; a form that asks too little may make follow-up difficult. Decide how the lead will be contacted, how consent and privacy are handled, and who owns the response.",
    "### Image and other assets",
    "Some campaigns support additional image, promotion, price or other asset types. Use them only when the format fits the campaign and the information is accurate. Images should be genuine, clear and appropriate to the offer. Promotions need valid dates and terms. Price information should match the destination and should not create a misleading expectation.",
    "### Organise assets by intent",
    "Assets work best when the campaign theme is clear. A campaign for WordPress development should not use sitelinks that send people to unrelated social-media services. Keep destinations aligned with the keyword theme and the ad's promise. Where the account supports campaign or ad-group associations, use the level that makes the asset relevant to the intended audience.",
    "### A practical asset review",
    "- Check that every sitelink opens the correct page and works on mobile.\n- Use distinct destinations rather than duplicate links.\n- Keep callouts factual, short and non-repetitive.\n- Use structured snippets to describe actual offerings.\n- Test the phone number and lead-form follow-up process.\n- Review image and promotion assets for accuracy and policy compliance.\n- Compare asset-level reporting where available, but do not assume correlation proves causation.\n- Revisit assets when the service, page or campaign objective changes.",
    "### Common mistakes",
    "The most common mistakes are repeating the same message across every asset, sending all links to the homepage, using unsupported claims, adding expired promotions, or creating call and lead options that the business cannot handle. An asset is part of the customer journey. If the destination or follow-up is broken, extra visibility can make the problem worse.",
    "For help structuring a Google Ads campaign, see [My DP Digital's paid campaign service](https://www.mydpdigital.in/services/paid-social-ads), review the [services overview](https://www.mydpdigital.in/services), or [send a campaign enquiry](https://www.mydpdigital.in/contact).",
    "### Sources",
    "[Google Ads: Use as many asset types as possible](https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/12073962?hl=en) and [About assets](https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7331111?hl=en). Check the live Help pages for current formats, eligibility and limits before making account changes."
  ],
